<section class="section" id="boundary-method" aria-labelledby="boundary-method-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
    <h2 id="boundary-method-heading">How the lines were built, and how far each one can be trusted</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Every reserve and zone line on these maps is taken from a Rajasthan Forest Department map file, not from
      OpenStreetMap. The Department's register says these files are for management use, are not final and are not
      for legal purposes; the legal boundary is the notification schedules. About
      {{ stats.not_mapped_notified_km2 | round }} sq km of the 1,135.787 sq km notified in 2023 has no outline of its
      own in any public map file (about 72 sq km of it lies in forest blocks that the district files draw only
      whole), so no outline here, or anywhere public, shows the whole reserve.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <ol class="method-steps">
      <li><strong>Fixed inputs.</strong> Each Forest Department file is stored with its SHA-256 checksum; the build stops if a file changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Reserve outline.</strong> The four park and sanctuary files, the reserve file and the 2023 whole-block buffer additions are joined. Slivers under 0.01 sq km where two files' edges miss each other are filled.</li>
      <li><strong>Reserve-and-ESZ outline.</strong> Both ESZ files are added. Holes the official files draw are kept; gaps under 0.5 sq km between files are filled.</li>
      <li><strong>ESZ bands.</strong> Each band is its ESZ file minus the reserve, park and sanctuary files, so no land is counted as both reserve and ESZ apart from a 0.02 sq km sliver of the Rawtha block.</li>
      <li><strong>Core and buffer, approximately.</strong> No official file separates core from buffer. The 2013 core is approximated by the national park, Darrah and Jawahar Sagar files, used whole, and the buffer by the rest of the reserve file. Used whole, these files fill {{ stats.unit_files_in_reserve_file_holes_km2 | km2 }} sq km of holes in the reserve file (nearly all the village of Girdharpura, which the Tiger Conservation Plan lists as a core village) and add {{ stats.unit_files_outside_reserve_file_km2 | km2 }} sq km around Borabas that only the Jawahar Sagar file draws.</li>
      <li><strong>Drawing.</strong> Maps use one Web Mercator scale on both axes, so shapes keep their proportions; areas are measured on the WGS84 ellipsoid. Rivers, roads, places and districts come from one fixed OpenStreetMap snapshot (Overture Maps release 2026-10-21.0).</li>
      <li><strong>Reproducible.</strong> The scripts are <code>scripts/build-boundary-layers.py</code> and <code>scripts/build-gis-maps.py</code> in this site's <a href="https://github.com/vinaychittora/mhtr">open repository</a>; <code>Data/source-materials/gis/README.md</code> lists every input. The credit watermark and embedded authorship are written by the same script.</li>
    </ol>
    <aside class="method-aside card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Read the areas with care</p>
      <p>
        "Drawn" is what the public files add up to. "Notified" is the figure a notification prints, or our sum of its
        hectare rows where it prints none; those sums are marked "by our sum" wherever they appear. A gap between the
        two is a limit of the files, not a finding about the reserve.
      </p>
      <a class="button-link button-link-secondary" href="/boundaries-and-notifications/">Notified areas, dated</a>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <h3>Measured against notified</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Layer</th>
