  <div class="source-panel" id="esz-evidence">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Reading the ESZ file</p>
      <h3>MHTR.kmz is an outer envelope, not the eco-sensitive zone</h3>
      <p>
        The register's <strong>MHTR.kmz</strong>, listed under “ESZ KML as on 01.06.2026,” records the
        <em>outer limit</em> around the 2013 reserve and its eco-sensitive zone. Its 1,003.91 sq km (WGS84, after
        subtracting the inner ring) is roughly the 2013 reserve (759.99 sq km) plus the notified ESZ (248.70 sq km),
        so it must not be quoted as the ESZ area, and it does not show the 2023 extent: the Bhainsrorgarh core added
        on 5 October 2023 lies outside it and has its own 2021 ESZ file, <strong>BHAINSROAD ESZ.kmz</strong>.
      </p>
      <dl class="home-facts">
        <div><dt>Publisher</dt><dd>Rajasthan Forest Department</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Register date</dt><dd>ESZ KML as on 1 June 2026</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Source filename</dt><dd>MHTR.kmz / MHTR-ESZ.kml</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Geometry</dt><dd>A 3,546-point outer ring and a 61-point inner ring. The inner ring is a 3.53 sq km enclave north-east of Rawatbhata, around Jawara (Jaora) Khurd, that this file leaves out of the ESZ and no reserve file includes. The Gazette's map (Annexure-IIB, p. 37) draws the same loop, but its village list (Annexure-IV, S.No. 52, p. 45) names Jawara Khurd as an ESZ village, at a point inside the loop.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>SHA-256</dt><dd><code>fe784add4d17d84f<wbr>7bf7191c8b401312<wbr>f20a74ed91dc3d5d<wbr>546fb3d265a828ea</code></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Use limits</dt><dd>The Department's register marks its map files for management use, not final and not for legal purposes.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
